#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096504 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096706 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c990d69-54da-5541-948f-0d5eff96feb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the X-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "1456cdfb-6ae9-5439-8ebe-e5b41687dfa9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5337" :question/problem "![](images/7d/7d1bf0d7128d5e1fffab3f4323f4f4c3ef3b7cae0e041a0cb2a510045d570f40.png)\n\n \n\nWhat is the $x$-intercept of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. The diagram shows that the parabola has a vertex at $(14,2)$ and passes through the point $\\left(12,6\\right).$\n\nThe equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^2 = 4p(x-h).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k) = (14,2)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-2)^2 = 4p(x-14).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(12,6),$ we substitute $x=12$ and $y=6$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6-2)^{2} & =4p(12-14) \\\\ 16 & =4p(-2) \\\\ p & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =-8(x-14).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y= 0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =-8(x-14) \\\\ (0-2)^{2} & =-8(x-14) \\\\ 4 & =-8(x-14) \\\\ x-14 & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}+14 \\\\ x & =\\frac{27}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $\\dfrac{27}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096505 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddc6c4b1-b4c4-52f8-9f25-feda3130e358" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "77303537-c614-5269-a22e-0bd412e7fa99" :question/math-academy-id "e-5339" :question/problem "Find the points of intersection of the parabola $(y-2)^2=4(x+1)$ with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the points of intersection of the parabola with the $y$-axis, we set $x=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4(x+1) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =4(0+1) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ y-2 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ y & =2\\pm 2 \\\\ y & =0,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parabola intersects the $y$-axis $(0,0)$ and $(0,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096506 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "4ce7b936-de41-5aeb-b980-80d9fa91314f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5340" :question/problem "![](images/3b/3b08115f7ede4b4caf8704e893ec48ec78d01870ac3df86a691d47f51ba832d8.png)\n\n  \n\nWhat are the $y$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. The diagram shows that the parabola has a vertex at $(7,-4)$ and passes through the point $\\left(5,-6\\right).$\n\nThe equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^2=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k) = (7,-4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y+4)^2=4p(x-7).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(5,-6),$ we substitute $x=5$ and $y=-6$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6+4)^{2} & =4p(5-7) \\\\ 4 & =4p(-2) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+4)^{2}=-2(x-7).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x= 0.$ So, we substitute $x= 0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+4)^{2} & =-2(0-7) \\\\ (y+4)^{2} & =14 \\\\ y+4 & =\\pm \\sqrt{14} \\\\ y & =-4\\pm \\sqrt{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $-4-\\sqrt{14}$ and $-4+\\sqrt{14}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a12aea1c-c3b3-575b-9749-72d8629fcb98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094676} {:answer/id #uuid "bbb23db4-408f-5c84-8753-c37ba86e241f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094677} {:answer/id #uuid "c845e4a4-ad56-58de-a58b-fe5cd1d78e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094678} {:answer/id #uuid "15359918-1f3e-5d67-954c-1cfd40ba2d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094679} {:answer/id #uuid "6ce42d7a-d8b5-5cac-ba6d-014c71515c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15359918-1f3e-5d67-954c-1cfd40ba2d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094679} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9bd1337-0a5a-55ad-b855-c3e655576ec6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d128bb5c-b2a0-55d9-a9bb-7094e5734308" :question/math-academy-id "q-14242" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d457bfe2ac8b0a86da15e2c34704ae5011a58ad8c148905aa1df12a674544f6c.png)\n\nWhat are the $y$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-9,5)$ and passes through the point $(7,1).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(-9,5)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-5)^{2}=4p(x+9).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(7,1),$ we substitute $x=7$ and $y=1$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1-5)^{2} & =4p(7+9) \\\\ 16 & =4p(16) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-5)^{2} & =(x+9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-5)^{2} & =(0+9) \\\\ (y-5)^{2} & =9 \\\\ y-5 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =5\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =2,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $2$\nand $8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddc6c4b1-b4c4-52f8-9f25-feda3130e358" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b4bc6f-6eae-58f3-bd4e-a6df1a6e19f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,8\\sqrt{2})$ and $(0,-8\\sqrt{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441392} {:answer/id #uuid "549b8f34-438a-55fd-81c8-cc57361533ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(8\\sqrt{3},0)$ and $(-8\\sqrt{3},0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441402} {:answer/id #uuid "75638e1e-4fe2-5380-80d6-466225c94b00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,8\\sqrt{5})$ and $(0,-8\\sqrt{5})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441406} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea9ba4a-4b8b-5bad-a45e-00c2f7caa19d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,4\\sqrt{7})$ and $(0,-4\\sqrt{7})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441407} {:answer/id #uuid "c47ad941-a4b2-5c3c-af7a-07dadfbe2daa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,4\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,-4\\sqrt{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05b4bc6f-6eae-58f3-bd4e-a6df1a6e19f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,8\\sqrt{2})$ and $(0,-8\\sqrt{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441392} :answer-field/id #uuid "f11adb4f-a6df-5572-a829-d227a29ca6f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5da328f-bab5-5074-ba79-45d8e6dffb79" :question/math-academy-id "q-14280" :question/problem "Find the points of intersection of the parabola ${y}^{2}=-8(x-16)$ with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "When the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we set $x=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =-8(0-16) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =128 \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{128} \\\\ y & =\\pm 8\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parabola intersects the $y$-axis at $(0,8\\sqrt{2})$ and $(0,-8\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096706 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c990d69-54da-5541-948f-0d5eff96feb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the X-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23b9a138-ccef-5b44-bdde-523564d8cad9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\sqrt{5}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441397} {:answer/id #uuid "26111051-d566-5d79-af8c-be170c78e32b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\frac{21}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441398} {:answer/id #uuid "3e9d1839-ab38-5603-9316-98bd943923cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\sqrt{5}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441400} {:answer/id #uuid "cb0b6c80-8dbc-56a7-a1a0-d14593c8129b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\sqrt{3}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441417} {:answer/id #uuid "d6831979-452b-5430-b4b7-e4fc46bb3bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6831979-452b-5430-b4b7-e4fc46bb3bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441418} :answer-field/id #uuid "95e16fd0-a1f9-576d-8004-2a0b51672b20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "165f408f-fdbf-512c-a0a1-ee39f4be587e" :question/math-academy-id "q-165432" :question/problem "![](images/db/dbf4056d9e2709bd959f4b8b79168941247af16ed1ae1b5dceb716cabe424830.png)\n\nWhat are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(3,4)$ and passes through the point $(2,3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(3,4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4p(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(2,3),$ we substitute $x=2$ and $y=3$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-3)^{2} & =4p(3-4) \\\\ 1 & =4p(-1) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =-(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =-(0-4) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{4} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $1$ and $5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddc6c4b1-b4c4-52f8-9f25-feda3130e358" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ec967a6-45c9-582d-b788-eca41cd0c25a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ec967a6-45c9-582d-b788-eca41cd0c25a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441405} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f4b1bc8-53fc-58ca-a338-90cc2e17cebf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441430} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0153ff6d-d05a-56ce-9b58-02c01757d0ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0153ff6d-d05a-56ce-9b58-02c01757d0ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441391} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7e72b22-0ede-5974-9f47-a89f67fcf4d2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "af6bef8d-9bda-5865-8318-f03b8c8ed1c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-251434" :question/problem "The parabola ${y}^{2}-3x+12y+36=0$ intersects the $x$-axis at the point$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "When the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-3x+12y+36 & =0 \\\\ (0)^{2}-3x+12(0)+36 & =0 \\\\ -3x+36 & =0 \\\\ -3x & =-36 \\\\ x & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parabola intersects the $x$-axis at $(12,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddc6c4b1-b4c4-52f8-9f25-feda3130e358" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15ab6d36-955b-5319-b4c2-4ceabe2971b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15ab6d36-955b-5319-b4c2-4ceabe2971b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441393} :answer-field/id #uuid "1be16104-19f9-514e-9edd-dc5c316b5114" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441421} {:answer-field/choices 