my-id "q-251439" :question/problem "**From top to bottom**, the points of intersection of the parabola ${y}^{2}-12x-2y-8=0$ with the $y$-axis are $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of $A$ and $B.$\n\n$A(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)\\,$ and $\\,B(${{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "When the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we set $x=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-12(0)-2y-8 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y-8 & =0 \\\\ (y+2)(y-4) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the intersection points, from top to bottom, are $(0,4)$ and $(0,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096706 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c990d69-54da-5541-948f-0d5eff96feb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the X-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b18a44d7-d4b0-55cb-86af-0e13704b8df9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b18a44d7-d4b0-55cb-86af-0e13704b8df9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376985} :answer-field/id #uuid "44eeaa7e-95d0-594d-b1b9-89222d742f44" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ed7cc33-cf3b-5b34-a2ab-03c46db15415" :question/math-academy-id "q-251694" :question/problem "![](images/7e/7e3d4078888350baccee76e73ab9b4a80d2ec95359fbe965bef3dd6a07e5c3b5.png)\n\nThe value of the $x$-intercept of the parabola shown above is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-6,4)$ and passes through the point $(-4,8).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-6,4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}=4p(x+6).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-4,8),$ we substitute $x=-4$ and $y=8$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8-4)^{2} & =4p(-4+6) \\\\ (4)^{2} & =4p\\cdot (2) \\\\ 16 & =8p \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =8(x+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-4)^{2} & =8(x+6) \\\\ 16 & =8(x+6) \\\\ 2 & =x+6 \\\\ x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096706 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c990d69-54da-5541-948f-0d5eff96feb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the X-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b4f84fb-e01c-5de9-8a75-f717912ae5de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b4f84fb-e01c-5de9-8a75-f717912ae5de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376988} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3a3dacb-4323-51ba-b4c3-d89cd23af82b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "05525262-11b5-5c22-9660-edbf983c26e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-251697" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd628bef568023d079cff41ed68efae949a0259822d79273671f633036e743ca.png)\n\nThe value of the $x$-intercept of the parabola shown above is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(3,2)$ and passes through the point $(21,-4).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(3,2)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n{(y-2)}^{2}=4p(x-3).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(21,-4),$ we substitute $x=21$ and $y=-4$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-4-2)}^{2} & =4p(21-3) \\\\ {(-6)}^{2} & =4p\\cdot (18) \\\\ 36 & =72p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =2(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-2)^{2} & =2(x-3) \\\\ 4 & =2(x-3) \\\\ 2 & =x-3 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096706 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c990d69-54da-5541-948f-0d5eff96feb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the X-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab9359e1-2230-572b-b145-165bd7eeb22a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab9359e1-2230-572b-b145-165bd7eeb22a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291377} :answer-field/id #uuid "d38bb03c-5f69-54cf-9a38-955ba1cb544f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292378} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f83b49c-5263-5a91-8e87-dd8dccdaf781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f83b49c-5263-5a91-8e87-dd8dccdaf781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291324} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5adab7c-e4c7-5d86-8e1b-e14e115b5f92" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3651a6f9-db8c-55ba-9b38-b5a076f97109" :question/math-academy-id "q-251784" :question/problem "![](images/af/afb7abacdffafd634165fd1f35868878b2bc420df6a173b48f6a17c7638f7dec.png)\n\n**From left to right**, the **exact values** of the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above are{{field-1}}\nand \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-3,4)$ and passes through the point $(0,-5).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-3,4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =4p(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(0,-5),$ we substitute $x=0$ and $y=-5$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0+3)^{2} & =4p(-5-4) \\\\ 9 & =4p(-9) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =-(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =-(0-4) \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x+3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{4} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are $-5$  and $-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292534 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf229ea4-be5b-5001-9fc2-49258187ce94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf229ea4-be5b-5001-9fc2-49258187ce94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291453} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7860a9b-7728-5971-a35a-bae3745118ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0acb67a-a7e6-5061-89d3-c3088fc5fb2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-251796" :question/problem "![](images/ce/cec5eacc688d3c3a619aeb6ede2b1c9f9290b8045699259d4c1d933ecefd1835.png)\n\nThe value of the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above  is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(4,-6)$ and passes through the point $(-2,3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(4,-6)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x-4)^{2}=4p(y+6).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-2,3),$ we substitute $x=-2$ and $y=3$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2-4)^{2} & =4p(3+6) \\\\ 36 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4(y+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-4)^{2} & =4(y+6) \\\\ 16 & =4(y+6) \\\\ 4 & =y+6 \\\\ y & =4-6 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "862218b9-e397-5b8f-8db7-eedde7bba86f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "862218b9-e397-5b8f-8db7-eedde7bba86f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441409} :answer-field/id #uuid "816b594c-0ba8-5f8d-b781-18f390478cc1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441426} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "199f0fce-6ad5-5b52-bb36-a5c2e347b81d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "199f0fce-6ad5-5b52-bb36-a5c2e347b81d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441395} :answer-field/id #uuid "da43aee3-09a1-5940-8ad9-f3914ca15293" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4260057c-f969-5185-9095-04c56e87e760" :question/math-academy-id "q-251823" :question/problem "![](images/15/1500cb3f15e5a32229c861a7b5dcc19e06de0cd6757c8a872f5eb14ad6cb32de.png)\n\n**From top to bottom**, the **exact** values of the $y$-intercepts of the parabola shown above are{{field-1}}\nand \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-1,4)$ and passes through the point $(3,0).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(-1,4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}=4p(x+1).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(3,0),$ we substitute $x=3$ and $y=0$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-4)^{2} & =4p(3+1) \\\\ 16 & =4p(4) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =4(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =4(0+1) \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =4 \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2+4 \\\\ y & =2,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are $6$ and  $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3326bfbc-602b-5b97-a232-910eb4540d34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3326bfbc-602b-5b97-a232-910eb4540d34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441399} :answer-field/id #uuid "78edd9c5-7539-5d54-8eb1-437fb325e3d3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441425} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1fd71a3-f959-5632-b94a-c770e9c6d2a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1fd71a3-f959-5632-b94a-c770e9c6d2a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441414} :answer-field/id #uuid "91e3b777-9db3-51a5-ab92-8bddd54cd065" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a25c3b40-7f3b-57d8-a341-b7f66aac34c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-251826" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e95f9087b5b607f2d33d49c0f5db123eede589e53adc94747f3a33c1fe68f90.png)\n\n**From top to bottom**, the **exact** values of the $y$-intercepts of the parabola shown above are{{field-1}}\nand \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-8,-1)$ and passes through the point $(-6,1).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(-8,-1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y+1)^{2}=4p(x+8).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-6,1),$ we substitute $x=-6$ and $y=1$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+1)^{2} & =4p(-6+8) \\\\ 4 & =4p(2) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =2(x+8).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =2(0+8) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =16 \\\\ y+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{16} \\\\ y & =\\pm 4-1 \\\\ y & =-5,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are $3$ and  $-5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddc6c4b1-b4c4-52f8-9f25-feda3130e358" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "161002e7-3475-55d1-bf33-13e031fadd4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-27,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441394} {:answer/id #uuid "4134ea6b-accb-5265-8339-c07c21944ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,27)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441401} {:answer/id #uuid "a5b77d34-ec2b-5142-964b-faad40063dd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(27,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441411} {:answer/id #uuid "ace6d14a-2a71-536b-8bbd-cd9914aff9ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-9,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441412} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf67998-b0d8-57b1-bbac-1c4711ac29d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "161002e7-3475-55d1-bf33-13e031fadd4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-27,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441394} :answer-field/id #uuid "58b63720-0427-5c5f-80b8-922ecf25b780" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "906c2725-0c67-5b11-96ab-a36547c9a017" :question/math-academy-id "q-73764" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the parabola\n$(y-9)^{2}=-3x$\nwith the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "When the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-9)^{2} & =-3x \\\\ 81 & =-3x \\\\ x & =-27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parabola intersects the $x$-axis at $(-27,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342087 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096706 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c990d69-54da-5541-948f-0d5eff96feb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the X-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23d76beb-d71a-5dfa-94a8-e101972d6f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\sqrt{6}-1$ and $-\\sqrt{6}+1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339439} {:answer/id #uuid "2d5a1f0b-7284-5bbc-b1e7-2ff8ad7d7d30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4$ and $-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339524} {:answer/id #uuid "37cbab4f-5a5d-53a0-989a-2bba919f1c6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{13}{3}$ and $-\\frac{5}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339629} {:answer/id #uuid "444fa7d9-40fd-53f5-9b43-5cab17eacd5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{7}{2}$ and $-\\frac{5}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339731} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a5235b-49d2-541d-b7c7-7db7eaa4f152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-5$ and $-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d5a1f0b-7284-5bbc-b1e7-2ff8ad7d7d30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4$ and $-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339524} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a6157a6-9ea9-521a-b21f-1bebe23c029e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9fb00539-5721-5d8a-80d7-0dcf285c8845" :question/math-academy-id "q-73831" :question/problem "![](images/0a/0a9d728a5927f380bbc88445c6a409974d94050040f23000cce50f70de436917.png)\n\nWhat are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-3,-1)$ and passes through the point $(-6,8).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-3,-1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =4p(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-6,8),$ we substitute $x=-6$ and $y=8$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6+3)^{2} & =4p(8+1) \\\\ 9 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =(0+1) \\\\ x+3 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $-4$ and $-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2021e1ee-a186-5932-a314-361aeb983eb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441396} {:answer/id #uuid "9e728a1e-a0dc-5359-8857-cc5f28c3dca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441410} {:answer/id #uuid "bf011f4b-fdf9-543e-b412-3cda9fe4fcd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441413} {:answer/id #uuid "c7aaabf2-9b3d-50fb-a39a-20b69f5186a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441416} {:answer/id #uuid "faf3532c-56de-5ea6-8e58-fe9e6cec5cac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e728a1e-a0dc-5359-8857-cc5f28c3dca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441410} :answer-field/id #uuid "9132e75d-f34a-5018-bfed-a7561864c4ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "baa62b58-d974-5e44-ba9b-341e234529fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-73837" :question/problem "![](images/19/19895cff608c6c9fce2598b235b5415ac2856ccf508f11df52545672918ad6f7.png)\n\nWhat is the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-2,-5)$ and passes through the point $(1,4).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(-2,-5)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}=4p(y+5).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(1,4),$ we substitute $x=1$ and $y=4$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+2)^{2} & =4p(4+5) \\\\ 9 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2} & =y+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0+2)^{2} & =y+5 \\\\ 4 & =y+5 \\\\ y & =4-5 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342092 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddc6c4b1-b4c4-52f8-9f25-feda3130e358" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18731f91-5a2b-5b17-a387-eb4cc9a4d188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,-\\sqrt{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339346} {:answer/id #uuid "20d59ae8-b207-56f8-a181-059ef32b89f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no intersection points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339410} {:answer/id #uuid "b3e77b72-b0aa-5aa2-80c3-74d69e6d1bde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,\\sqrt{6})$ and $(0,-\\sqrt{6})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340763} {:answer/id #uuid "e98d2c48-e98a-5bd8-bf7e-745db4c2794a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,\\sqrt{6})$ and $(0,-2\\sqrt{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341189} {:answer/id #uuid "f0c3edd0-3761-5700-a2bb-55bbe3d60703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,-2\\sqrt{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20d59ae8-b207-56f8-a181-059ef32b89f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no intersection points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339410} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1b9401a-2720-53ab-b421-d5879f6deca9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6253cbd5-f5b5-5898-ac4f-5142a7aaca73" :question/math-academy-id "q-73847" :question/problem "Find the points of intersection of the parabola ${y}^{2}=3(x-2)$ with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "When the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we set $x=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =3(0-2) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the equation ${y}^{2}=-6$ has no solutions, we conclude that there are no intersection points."}]}
